  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper level low pressure will continue to spin near the Canadian\nMaritimes through Labor Day, which will maintain a trough over\nthe northeast CONUS. Drier air will continue to filter in from\nthe north through the afternoon and night, with the CWA dropping\nbelow 1.0 inch precipitable water values by 12Z Sunday.\n\nAhead of the dewpoint discontinuity, scattered showers and a few\nthunderstorms are forecast to develop, mainly to the south of\nUS-422. Here, some breaks in the cloud cover should allow for\nmixed-layer CAPE in the 500 to 1000 J/kg range. Although lapse\nrates aloft are poor, updrafts may become tall enough for\nlightning generation in some cases. The presence of 25-30 knots\nof shear may allow for some loose storm organization as the \nafternoon wears on, mainly south of I-70. However, the lack of \nbetter instability and the weak lapse rates should largely \nnegate the threat of strong to severe storms. SPC agrees, \nkeeping the day 1 Marginal risk area to our south. PWAT values,\nalthough dropping, do remain near or just above 1.5 inch for the\nfavored area, so downpours remain possible. Will have to watch\nwet areas from the morning MCS, but only very isolated and\nrelatively minor water issues are anticipated right now.\n\nThe diurnal loss of instability will allow convection to largely\nwrap up just after sunset. Another round of patchy fog is\npossible by sunrise, especially in locations that received/will\nreceive rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe remainder of the holiday weekend will provide a needed break\nfrom the convective parade our region has seen lately. Surface\nhigh pressure settling in from southeast Canada, with\nnortheasterly surface low. A good deal of sunshine is forecast\nboth Sunday and Labor Day, with temperatures remaining at\nseasonable levels.\n\n500mb heights rise after Monday as the northeast trough opens\nand departs, and as central CONUS ridging begins to build across\nthe Ohio Valley. This will lead to a warming trend for Tuesday\nand Wednesday, with continued dry weather. The next appreciable\nchance for rain may arrive with a cold front during the\nWednesday night/Thursday period.",
